                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} John Isbell
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Locales: A "generalized topological space" or "pointless spaces" defined by a lattice (a complete [[Heyting algebra
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/locale+%28mathematics%29"} ?
                  also called a Brouwer lattice) just as for a topological space the open subsets form a lattice. If the lattice possess enough points it is a topological space. Locales are the main objects of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointless+topology"} pointless topology
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frames
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1972
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Formal theory of monads
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Formal+theory+of+monads"} ?
                  \: The theory of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monads
                  in 2-categories
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1972
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"} Peter Freyd
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Fundamental theorem of topos theory
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Fundamental+theorem+of+topos+theory"} ?
                  \: Every slice category (E,Y) of a topos E is a topos and the functor f
                  %em :(E,X)→(E,Y) preserves exponentials and the subobject classifier object Ω and has a right and left adjoint functor
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1972
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} Universes
                  for sets
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1972
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"}> Jean Bénabou
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmos"} Cosmoses
                  which categorize
                  = succeed "s:" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1972
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"}> Operads
                  \: An abstraction of the family of composable functions of several variables together with an action of permutation of variables. Operads can be seen as algebraic theories and algebras over operads are then models of the theories. Each operad gives a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                  on Top.
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} Multicategories
                  with one object are operads.
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      PROP
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/PRO+%28category+theory%29"} ?
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opetope"} opetope
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1972
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  William Mitchell-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"} Jean Bénabou
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell-Benabou+language"} Mitchell-Bénabou language
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Chris Reedy
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"}> Reedy categories
                  \: Categories of “shapes” that can be used to do homotopy theory. A Reedy category is a category R equipped with a structure enabling the inductive construction of diagrams and natural transformations of shape R. The most important consequence of a Reedy structure on R is the existence of a model structure on the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
                  M
                  %sup R
                  whenever M is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                  and hence a category. The opposite R° of a Reedy category R is a Reedy category. The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
                  Δ and more generally for any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                  X its category of simplices Δ/X is a Reedy category. The model structure on M
                  %sup Δ
                  for a model category M is described in an unpublished manuscript by Chris Reedy
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"}> Kenneth Brown
                  \-Stephen Gersten
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Shows the existence of a global closed
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model structure
                  on the categegory of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sheaf"} simplicial sheaves
                  on a topological space, with weak asumptions on the topological space
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"} Kenneth Brown
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Generalized sheaf cohomology
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Generalized+sheaf+cohomology"} ?
                  of a topological space X with coefficients a sheaf on X with values in Kans
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    category of spectra
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Spectrum+%28homotopy+theory%29"} ?
                  with some finiteness conditions. It generalizes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory#cohomology+theories"} generalized cohomology theory
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
                  with coefficients in a complex of abelian sheaves
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Finds that Cauchy completeness can be expressed for general
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    enriched categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Enriched+category"} ?
                  with the
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    category of generalized metric spaces
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/category+of+generalized+metric+spaces"} ?
                  as a special case. Cauchy sequences become left adjoint modules and convergence become representability
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"} Jean Bénabou
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Distributors
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Profunctor"} ?
                  (also called modules, profunctors,
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      directed bridges
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Categorical_bridge"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"} Pierre Deligne
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Proves the last of the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+conjectures"} Weil conjectures
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} John Boardman-Rainer Vogt
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"}> Segal categories
                  \: Simplicial analogues of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} A
                  %sub
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} ?
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} -categories
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+category"} simplicial categories
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1973
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Daniel Quillen
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+categories"}> Frobenius categories
                  \: An
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
                  in which the classes of injective and projective objects coincide and for all objects x in the category there is a deflation P(x)→x (the projective cover of x) and an inflation x→I(x) (the injective hull of x) such that both P(x) and I(x) are in the category of pro/injective objects. A Frobenius category E is an example of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                  and the quotient E/P (P is the class of projective/injective objects) is its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                  hE
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Artin"} Michael Artin
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Generalizes
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Deligne-Mumford stacks
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Algebraic+stack"} ?
                  to
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Artin stacks
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Algebraic+stack"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Robert Paré
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Paré monadicity theorem
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Par%C3%A9+monadicity+theorem"} ?
                  \: E is a topos→E° is monadic over E
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Andy Magid
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Generalizes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck%27s+Galois+theory"} Grothendiecks Galois theory
                  from groups to the case of rings using Galois groupoids
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"} Jean Bénabou
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Logic of
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    fibred categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Fibred+category"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    John Gray
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/John+Gray+%28mathematician%29"} ?
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+categories"} Gray categories
                  with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"} Kenneth Brown
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Writes a very influential paper that defines
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Browns categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/brown+category"} ?
                  of fibrant objects and dually Brown categories of cofibrant objects
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1974
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shiing-Shen+Chern"}> Shiing-Shen Chern
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Simons"} James Simons
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"}> Chern-Simons theory
                  \: A particular TQFT which describe knot and manifold invariants, at that time only in 3D
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1975
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saul+Kripke"}> Saul Kripke
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke-Joyal+semantics"} Kripke-Joyal semantics
                  of the
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Mitchell-Bénabou internal language
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Mitchell-Benabou+internal+language"} ?
                  for toposes: The logic in categories of sheaves is first order intuitionistic predicate logic
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1975
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Radu Diaconescu
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu+theorem"}> Diaconescu theorem
                  \: The internal axiom of choice holds in a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                  → the topos is a boolean topos. So in IZF the axiom of choice implies the law of excluded middle
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1975
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Manfred Szabo
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Polycategories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Polycategories"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1975
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Observes that
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Delignes theorem
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Deligne+theorem+%28disambiguation%29"} ?
                  about enough points in a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+topos"} coherent topos
                  implies the
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Gödel completeness theorem
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/G%C3%B6del%27s+completeness+theorem"} ?
                  for first order logic in that topos
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1976
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Schematic homotopy type
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Schematic+homotopy+type"} ?
                  s
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1976
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Marcel Crabbe
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+categories"} Heyting categories
                  also called
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      logoses
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/logos+%28mathematics%29"} ?
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Regular categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Regular+category"} ?
                  in which the subobjects of an object form a lattice, and in which each inverse image map has a right adjoint. More precisely a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
                  C such that for all morphisms f:A→B in C the functor f
                  %em
                    \:Sub
                    %sub> C
                    (B)→Sub
                    %sub> C
                    (A) has a left adjoint and a right adjoint. Sub
                    %sub> C
                    (A) is the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
                    of subobjects of A (the full subcategory of C/A whose objects are subobjects of A) in C. Every
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                    is a logos. Heyting categories generalize
                    = succeed "s." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1976
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Computads
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Computads"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1977
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"} Peter Johnstone
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Very influential book “Topos theory” (circulated as a preprint a year earlier).
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1977
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"}> Michael Makkai
                  \-Gonzalo Reyes
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Develops the
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Mitchell-Bénabou internal language
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Mitchell-Benabou+internal+language"} ?
                  of a topos thoroughly in a more general setting
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1977
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Andre Boileau-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"}> Andre Joyal
                  \-Jon Zangwill
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  LST
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      Local set theory
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Local+set+theory"} ?
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    typed set theory
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/typed+set+theory"} ?
                  whose underlying logic is higher order
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
                  = succeed "." do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      linguistic topos
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/linguistic+topos"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1977
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    John Roberts
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/John+Roberts+%28mathematician%29"} ?
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Introduces most general
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
                  of ω-categories with ω-categories as coefficients when he realized that general cohomology is about coloring simplices in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-categories
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+sheaf+cohomology"} nonabelian sheaf cohomology
                  in terms of
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    descent
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/descent+%28category+theory%29"} ?
                  for ω-category valued sheaves, and in terms of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+cohomology+theory"} homotopical cohomology theory
                  which realizes the cocycles. The two approaches are related by
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    codescent
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/descent+%28category+theory%29"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1978
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    John Roberts
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/John+Roberts+%28mathematician%29"} ?
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Complicial set
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Complicial+set"} ?
                  s (simplicial sets with structure or enchantment)
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1978
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Francois Bayen-Moshe Flato-Chris Fronsdal-
