                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Spin foam models
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Spin+foam"} ?
                  \: A 2-dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+complex"} cell complex
                  with faces labeled by representations and edges labeled by
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      intertwining operator
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/intertwining+operator"} ?
                  = succeed "." do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      spin network categories
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/spin+network+category"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1998
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} James Dolan
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcosm+principle"}> Microcosm principle
                  \: Certain algebraic structures can be defined in any category equipped with a categorified version of the same structure
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1998
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Alexander Rosenberg
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Alexander+Rosenberg+%28mathematician%29"} ?
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Noncommutative scheme
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Noncommutative+scheme"} ?
                  s: The pair (Spec(A),O
                  %sub> A
                  ) where A is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
                  and to it is associated a topological space Spec(A) together with a sheaf of rings O
                  %sub A
                  on it. In the case when A = QCoh(X) for X a scheme the pair (Spec(A),O
                  %sub> A
                  ) is naturally isomorphic to the scheme (X
                  %sup> Zar
                  ,O
                  %sub> X
                  ) using the equivalence of categories QCoh(Spec(R))=Mod
                  %sub> R
                  \. More generally abelian categories or triangulated categories or dg-categories or A
                  %sub> ∞
                  \-categories should be regarded as categories of quasicoherent sheaves (or complexes of sheaves) on noncommutative schemes. This is a starting point in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                  on noncommutative schemes and hence
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1998
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+category"}> Calabi-Yau categories
                  \: A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} linear category
                  with a trace map for each object of the category and an associated symmetric (with respects to objects) nondegenerate pairing to the trace map. If X is a smooth projective
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+variety"} Calabi-Yau variety
                  of dimension d then D
                  %sup> b
                  (Coh(X)) is a unital Calabi-Yau
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} A
                  %sub
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} ?
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} -category
                  of Calabi-Yau dimension d. A Calabi-Yau category with one object is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1999
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Bernstein"}> Joseph Bernstein
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Frenkel"}> Igor Frenkel
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Khovanov"} Mikhail Khovanov
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Temperley-Lieb categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Temperley-Lieb+categories"} ?
                  \: Objects are enumerated by nonnegative integers. The set of homomorphisms from object n to object m is a free R-module with a basis over a ring R. R is given by the isotopy classes of systems of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vert{n}\vert+\vert{m}\vert)/2
                  simple pairwise disjoint arcs inside a horizontal strip on the plane that connect in pairs
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert{n}\vert
                  points on the bottom and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert{m}\vert
                  points on the top in some order. Morphisms are composed by concatenating their diagrams. Temperley-Lieb categories are categorized
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Temperley-Lieb+algebra"} Temperley-Lieb algebra
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1999
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Moira Chas-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Sullivan"} Dennis Sullivan
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Constructs
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    String topology
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/String+topology"} ?
                  by cohomology. This is string theory on general topological manifolds
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1999
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Khovanov"} Mikhail Khovanov
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov+homology"}> Khovanov homology
                  \: A homology theory for knots such that the dimensions of the homology groups are the coefficients of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
                  of the knot
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1999
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Turaev"} Vladimir Turaev
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Homotopy quantum field theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HQFT"} HQFT
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1999
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                  \-Fabien Morel
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Constructs the
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    homotopy category of schemes
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/A%C2%B9+homotopy+theory"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1999
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Ronald Brown
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Ronald+Brown+%28mathematician%29"} ?
                  \-George Janelidze
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2-dimensional Galois theory
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2000
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Gives two constructions of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
                  of varieties, by model categories in homotopy theory and by a triangulated category of DM-motives
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2000
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yakov+Eliashberg"}> Yakov Eliashberg
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Givental"}> Alexander Givental
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Helmut+Hofer"} Helmut Hofer
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Floer+homology#Symplectic+field+theory+%28SFT%29"}> Symplectic field theory SFT
                  \: A functor Z from a geometric category of framed Hamiltonian structures and framed cobordisms between them to an algebraic category of certain differential D-modules and Fourier integral operators between them and satisfying some axioms
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2001
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Charles Rezk
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Constructs a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                  with certain generalized
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+category"} Segal categories
                  as the fibrant objects, thus obtaining a model for a homotopy theory of homotopy theories.
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      Complete Segal space
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Complete+Segal+space"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2001
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Charles Rezk
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Model topos
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Model+topos"} ?
                  es and their generalization
                  = succeed "es" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      homotopy topos
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/homotopy+topos"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2002
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Bertrand Toën-Gabriele Vezzosi
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Segal topos
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Segal+topos"} ?
                  es coming from
                  = succeed "," do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      Segal topologies
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Segal+topology"} ?
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Segal sites
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Segal+site"} ?
                  and stacks over them
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2002
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Bertrand Toën-Gabriele Vezzosi
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Homotopical algebraic geometry
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Homotopical+algebraic+geometry"} ?
                  \: The main idea is to extend
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    schemes
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/scheme+%28mathematics%29"} ?
                  by formally replacing the rings with any kind of “homotopy-ring-like object”. More precisely this object is a commutative monoid in a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
                  endowed with a notion of equivalences which are understood as “up-to-homotopy monoid” (e.g. E
                  %sub> ∞
                  \-rings)
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2002
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Peter Johnstone
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Peter+Johnstone+%28mathematician%29"} ?
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Influential book: sketches of an elephant - a topos theory compendium. It serves as an encyclopedia of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                  theory (2/3 volumes published as of 2008)
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2002
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Gaitsgory"}> Dennis Gaitsgory
                  \-Kari Vilonen-Edward Frenkel
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Proves the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+program"} geometric Langlands program
                  for GL(n) over finite fields
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2003
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Denis-Charles Cisinski
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Makes further work on
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    ABC model categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ABC+model+category"} ?
                  and brings them back into light. From then they are called ABC model categories after their contributors
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Gaitsgory"} Dennis Gaitsgory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Extended the proof of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+program"} geometric Langlands program
                  to include GL(n) over ‘’‘C’’’. This allows to consider curves over ‘’‘C’’‘ instead of over finite fields in the geometric Langlands program
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Mario Caccamo
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Formal
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    category theoretical expanded
                    = succeed "-calculus" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi λ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/category+theoretical+lambda+calculus"} ?
                  for categories
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Francis Borceux-Dominique Bourn
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Homological categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Homological+category"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  William Dwyer-Philips Hirschhorn-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"}> Daniel Kan
                  \-Jeffrey Smith
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Introduces in the book: Homotopy limit functors on model categories and homotopical categories, a formalism of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical categories
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+functor"} homotopical functors
                  (weak equivalence preserving functors) that generalize the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                  formalism of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Daniel Quillen
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    limit
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/limit+%28category+theory%29"} ?
                  and colimit functors (that are computed by local constructions in the book),
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    completeness
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Complete+category"} ?
                  and cocompleteness,
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjunction
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal properties
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Proves the
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Street-Roberts conjecture
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Street-Roberts+conjecture"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Definition of the descent weak ω-category of a cosimplicial weak ω-category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Characterization theorem for cosmoses
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Cosmos+%28mathematics%29"} ?
                  \: A bicategory M is a
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    cosmos
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cosmos+%28mathematics%29"} ?
                  iff there exists a base bicategory W such that M is biequivalent to Mod
                  %sub> W
                  \. W can be taken to be any full subbicategory of M whose objects form a small
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Cauchy generator
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Generator_%28category_theory%29"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                  \-Brian Day
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Quantum categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Quantum+categories"} ?
                  and
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      quantum groupoids
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/quantum+groupoids"} ?
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
                  V is an object R with an
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    opmorphism
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/opmorphism"} ?
                  h:R
                  %sup op
                  ⊗ R → A into a pseudomonoid A such that h
                  %sup *
                  is strong monoidal (preserves tensor product and unit up to coherent natural isomorphisms) and all R, h and A lie in the autonomous monoidal bicategory Comod(V)
                  %sup co
                  of comonoids. Comod(V)=Mod(V
                  %sup> op
                  )
                  %sup> coop
                  \. Quantum categories were introduced to generalize
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
                  with several objects
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Stephen Stolz
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Stephen+Stolz"} ?
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Definition of nD
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
                  of degree p parametrized by a manifold
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2004
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Stephen Stolz
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Stephen+Stolz"} ?
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
                  proposed in the 1980s to provide a geometric construction of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
                  (the precursor to
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      tmf
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Topological+modular+forms"} ?
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    TMF
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Topological+modular+forms"} ?
                  as a moduli space of supersymmetric Euclidean field theories. They conjectured a
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Stolz-Teichner picture
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Stolz-Teichner+picture"} ?
                  (analogy) between
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+filtration"} chromatic filtration
                  (de Rham cohomology,K-theory,Morava K-theories) and moduli spaces of supersymmetric QFTs parametrized by a manifold (proved in 0D and 1D)
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2005
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Peter Selinger
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Dagger categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Dagger+category"} ?
                  and
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+functor"} dagger functor
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    n-categories with duals
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/n-category+with+duals"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2005
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Peter Ozsváth
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Peter+Ozsv%C3%A1th"} ?
                  \-
