                    Zoltán Szabó
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Zolt%C3%A1n+Szab%C3%B3"} ?
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov+homology#Related+theories"} Knot Floer homology
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2006
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} P. Carrasco-A.R. Garzon-E.M. Vitale
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Categorical crossed module
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Categorical+crossed+module"} ?
                  s
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2006
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Aslak Buan-Robert Marsh-Markus Reineke-Idun Reiten-Gordana Todorov
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord>
                    Cluster categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Cluster+category"} ?
                  \: Cluster categories are a special case of triangulated
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+category"} Calabi-Yau categories
                  of Calabi-Yau dimension 2 and a generalization of
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cluster+algebra"} cluster algebra
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2006
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Monumental book: Higher topos theory: In its 940 pages Jacob Lurie generalize the common concepts of category theory to higher categories and defines
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      n-topose
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/n-topose"} ?
                  = succeed "es," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-topos"} ∞-topos
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves+of+n-types"} sheaves of n-types
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      ∞-site
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-site"} ?
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
                  and proves
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Lurie characterization theorem
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Lurie+characterization+theorem"} ?
                  for higher dimensional toposes. Lurie’s theory of higher toposes can be interpreted as giving a good theory of sheaves taking values in ∞-categories. Roughly an ∞-topos is an ∞-category which looks like the ∞-category of all
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
                  = succeed "." do
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      higher homotopy theory
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/higher+homotopy+theory"} ?
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    topos hypothesis
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/topos+hypothesis"} ?
                  is that the (n+1)-category nCat is a Grothendieck (n+1)-topos. Higher topos theory can also be used in a purely algebro-geometric way to solve various moduli problems in this setting
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2007
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Bernhard Keller-Thomas Hugh
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    d-cluster categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cluster+category"} ?
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2007
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Gaitsgory"}> Dennis Gaitsgory
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Presents a derived version of the geometric
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Satake+equivalence"} Satake equivalence
                  and formulates a geometric
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+duality"} Langlands duality
                  for
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+group"} quantum group
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+dual+group"} Langlands dual group
                  = succeed "G" do
                    %sup L
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perverse+sheaves"} perverse sheaves
                  (or
                  = succeed "s)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-module"} D-module
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmannian"} Grassmannian
                  Gr
                  %sub> G
                  \=G((t))/G[[t]] of the original group G
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2008
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"} Bruce Bartlett
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Primacy of the point hypothesis: An n-dimensional unitary extended TQFT is completely described by the n-Hilbert space it assigns to a point. This is a reformulation of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2008
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clemens+Berger"} Clemens Berger
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Extends and improved the definition of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
                  to become invariant under
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2008
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valery+Lunts"}> Valery Lunts
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Orlov"} Dmitri Orlov
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  The derived categories of coherent sheaves on quasiprojective varieties have unique
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} dg-enhancements
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2008
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Mike Hopkins-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} Detailed outline of proof of
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} Baez
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} Dolan
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+tangle+hypothesis"} tangle hypothesis
                  and
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} Baez
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} Dolan
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
                  which classify
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended TQFT
                  in all dimensions
